# pacman runs this before it removes the package. A machine that is still set
# up loses what re-seals the loader after an edit of limine.conf and repairs
# it after a Limine upgrade (docs/spec.md, failure table), so it is told. This
# only prints: it never fails and changes nothing.
pre_remove() {
  [[ ! -e /var/lib/omasecboot/enabled ]] || cat <<'NOTICE'
==> OmaSecBoot is still set up on this machine.
    Removing the package takes away what re-seals the loader after an edit
    of limine.conf and repairs it after a Limine upgrade. An edit of
    limine.conf then stops the loader, with Secure Boot on or off, and with
    Secure Boot on the next Limine upgrade can leave a loader that the
    firmware refuses.
    Reinstall the package. To return to stock, turn Secure Boot off and run
    "sudo omasecboot remove", then remove the package. With an encrypted
    Windows beside it, have its recovery key at hand before Secure Boot goes
    off: BitLocker can ask for it then.
NOTICE
}

# pacman runs this after it upgrades the package. On a machine that is set up
# with an ESP that users other than root can write, every pass seals and signs
# nothing (docs/spec.md, failure table), so it is told, by the installed
# library's own rule; a rule that cannot run counts as a mount it cannot read.
# This only prints: it never fails and changes nothing.
post_upgrade() {
  [[ -e /var/lib/omasecboot/enabled ]] || return 0
  ! bash -c 'source /usr/lib/omasecboot/common.sh && esp_mount_is_safe' >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  cat <<'NOTICE'
==> OmaSecBoot seals and signs nothing on this machine until the mount of
    its EFI system partition shows that only root can write it. Until then
    an edit of limine.conf stops the loader, with Secure Boot on or off, and
    with Secure Boot on a Limine upgrade can leave a loader that the
    firmware refuses. "sudo omasecboot status" says what to change.
NOTICE
}
